## OWASP Cross-Reference

Some of the Nikto findings can be related to the OWASP Top 10.

| Finding                      | Possible OWASP Category                                |
| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| Missing security headers     | A05 – Security Misconfiguration                        |
| PHP version disclosure       | A05 – Security Misconfiguration                        |
| Potential file disclosure    | A01 – Broken Access Control / A05                      |
| Exposed admin login          | A05 – Security Misconfiguration                        |
| Potential command execution  | A03 – Injection, if confirmed                          |
| Vulnerable/outdated software | A06 – Vulnerable and Outdated Components, if confirmed |

Not every Nikto finding fits directly into an OWASP category, so these mappings are only used as a general comparison.

---

## Conclusion

This task gave me practical experience using Nikto to scan a web server and interpret the results.

The scan identified several issues, including missing security headers, PHP version disclosure, possible system-file access and several suspicious PHP files.

One of the main things I learned from the scan is that automated tools do not always give a definite answer. Some findings need to be checked manually before they can be considered real vulnerabilities.

Using Nikto together with other tools such as Nmap can provide a better overall view of the security of a system.
